A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers, forwarding client requests and providing SSL termination, load balancing, and caching.

**Basic reverse proxy** (forward all traffic to a backend app):
```nginx
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.company.com;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;  # Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name app.company.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/app.company.com.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/app.company.com.key;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;  # Backend app
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        # WebSocket support
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    }
}
```

**Multiple backends** (route by path):
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name company.com;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/;  # API server
    }

    location /app/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000/;  # Web app
    }

    location / {
        root /var/www/html;  # Static files
    }
}
```

**Setup steps**:
```bash
# Install Nginx
sudo apt install nginx

# Create site config
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf

# Enable the site
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

# Test configuration
sudo nginx -t

# Reload
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

**Benefits of reverse proxying**:
- **SSL termination**: handle HTTPS at Nginx, backend uses HTTP.
- **Security**: hide backend server details, add rate limiting, block malicious requests.
- **Performance**: enable gzip compression, static file caching, connection pooling.
- **Flexibility**: change backend servers without changing the public URL.

**Rate limiting** (prevent abuse):
```nginx
http {
    limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=10r/s;
}
server {
    location /api/ {
        limit_req zone=api burst=20 nodelay;
        proxy_pass http://backend;
    }
}
```
